[Chapter "Key ideas: Why these setups"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Against the London you don't need a new opening. Keep the setup you already play against 1.d4, with a few changes.

If you play 1...d5 (the Queen's Gambit course), set up as in the Queen's Gambit Declined: ...Nf6, ...e6, ...Bd6 and castling, then ...c5, ...Qc7, ...Nbd7 and ...e5. Step through the moves below.

If you play 1...Nf6 and the King's Indian, keep that setup too: ...g6, ...Bg7, castling and ...d6. Then strike with ...e5 when White's bishop is on d3, or with ...c5 when it is on e2 (see Plans for both sides).

Why this works. White's setup is solid but slow. Without c4, White puts no pressure on d5, so you can develop in peace. You have three targets: the bishop on f4 (offer a trade with ...Bd6), the pawn on d4 (...c5 and ...e5) and the pawn on b2 that the bishop left behind (...Qb6). Stockfish rates the main lines of this course at about 0.0, equal; the player who knows the plans has the easier game.

How often: on Lichess, among players rated 1800 to 2199, White plays 2.Bf4 in about 18% of games after 1...d5 and 16% after 1...Nf6. Others reach the London with 2.Nf3 and 3.Bf4; the same plans apply.

The London takes its name from the London tournament of 1922, where several masters used it, and today it is one of the most popular openings at club level. Ding Liren won a game with it in his 2023 world championship match against Ian Nepomniachtchi.

Step through the moves to see the 1...d5 setup.}
1. d4 d5 2. Bf4 {This is the London System.} Nf6 {Develop first. ...c5 can wait a move.}
3. e3 e6 {[%csl Yc8] The Queen's Gambit Declined setup. The c8 bishop stays home for now and guards b7, so White's Qb3 tricks don't work.}
4. Nf3 Bd6 {[%cal Gd6f4] Offers to trade White's best minor piece. If 5.Bxd6, take back with the queen.}
5. Bg3 O-O 6. Nbd2 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4.}
7. c3 Qc7 {[%cal Gc7e5] The queen joins the fight for e5. White's knight can no longer settle on e5 easily, and ...e5 is coming.}
8. Bd3 Nbd7 {[%cal Gd7e5,Ge6e5] The knight covers e5 a third time. On d7 it is safe from Bb5, which could pin or trade a knight on c6. Next comes ...e5 once White has castled, or ...b6 and ...Bb7. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Plans for both sides"]
[Type "ideas"]
[FEN "r1b2rk1/ppqn1ppp/3bpn2/2pp4/3P4/2PBPNB1/PP1N1PPP/R2QK2R w KQ - 3 9"]

{[%cal Ge6e5,Gd6g3,Gb7b6,Gc8b7,Rf3e5,Re3e4,Rd3h7] [%csl Ye5] This is the position from the first page (Stockfish 0.0, equal). The green arrows are your plans, the red arrows White's.

Your plans. The square e5 is the battleground. With the queen on c7, the bishop on d6 and the knight on d7, you control it three times, so White can't plant a knight there. Then:
- ...e5 is the main break. After 9.O-O e5 10.dxe5 Nxe5, the London bishop has no targets and your pieces are free (Stockfish +0.2, equal).
- ...b6 and ...Bb7 develop the problem bishop. ...Ba6 to trade White's good bishop on d3 also works.
- ...Bxg3 at the right moment. It opens the h-file for White's rook, so it is safest once White has castled kingside, or when ...e5 follows at once.
- If White breaks first with 9.e4, take: 9...cxd4 10.cxd4 dxe4 11.Bxe4 Nxe4 12.Nxe4, and White's isolated d-pawn is a target (Stockfish −0.3, slightly better for Black).

White's plans. Ne5 with f4, the e4 break, and an attack on h7 with the d3 bishop. Your control of e5 takes the sting out of the first, and the second opens the center while your pieces are ready.

Against White's other setups with 1...d5:
- 5.Bxd6 Qxd6: castle, then ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...e5 (Stockfish +0.1, equal).
- 5.Bd3 Bxf4 6.exf4: White's pawns are doubled. Castle, then ...b6, ...c5, ...a5 and ...Ba6 to trade the light-squared bishops. Play ...a5 first when White's queen is on e2, or ...Ba6 drops the knight that takes back on a6 (Stockfish 0.0, equal).
- 5.Ne5: castle, then ...c5 and ...Qc7 to challenge the knight (Stockfish +0.1, equal).
- 4.c4: now it is a Queen's Gambit. Play 4...c5, as in the Queen's Gambit course (Stockfish 0.0, equal).

With 1...Nf6 and the King's Indian setup:
- Against Bd3 (5.Bd3 d6 6.c3 Nc6 7.Nbd2), play 7...e5. If 8.dxe5?? dxe5, you win a piece (see Tricks and traps). Stockfish: −0.2, equal.
- Against Be2 and h3, ...e5 is too early: dxe5 wins a pawn. Play ...c5 and ...Qb6 instead. After 7.c3 Qb6 8.Qb3, 8...Be6 hits the queen, and White has to trade or retreat (Stockfish −0.1, equal).
- Against 3.Nc3, play 3...d5. If White pushes h4, answer with ...h5 at once (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Pawn structures"]
[Type "ideas"]
[FEN "r1bq1rk1/ppp2pbp/2np1np1/4p3/3P1B2/2PBPN2/PP1N1PPP/R2QK2R w KQ - 0 8"]

{[%cal Ge5d4,Ge5e4,Gf8e8,Rf4g5,Rd4e5] [%csl Yd4,Ye5] Five structures come up again and again.

The tension (1...d5 lines). Your pawns on c5, d5 and e6 face White's triangle on c3, d4 and e3. Keep the tension until it helps you to release it. After ...cxd4 exd4, the e-file opens for White's rooks; after ...cxd4 cxd4, the c-file opens for yours.

After ...e5. This is your goal in the 1...d5 lines. Once the pawns are traded on e5, the London bishop has no targets and your pieces come alive. You may end up with an isolated pawn on d5; with active pieces that is a strength, not a weakness.

The reversed Carlsbad. If you take on d4 early and White takes back with the e-pawn (2...c5 3.e3 cxd4 4.exd4), the pawn structure is the Exchange Queen's Gambit, with you in White's shoes. Now you play the minority attack: ...b5 and ...b4 against White's pawn on c3. Develop the c8 bishop to f5 or g4 before ...e6.

The King's Indian structure (this position, after 2...g6 3.e3 Bg7 4.Nf3 O-O 5.Bd3 d6 6.c3 Nc6 7.Nbd2 e5). Your pawn on e5 hits d4. ...exd4 opens the e-file for a rook on e8 and the long diagonal for the g7 bishop. ...e4 gains space and can fork a knight on f3 and a bishop on d3, but the pawn can become a target once White plays f3 or c4. In general, if White trades on e5 and the queens come off, the game is symmetrical and you are fine. Here, though, 8.dxe5?? loses a piece (see Tricks and traps).

...c5 against the King's Indian London. When White's bishop is on e2, hit d4 with ...c5 instead of ...e5. After ...cxd4 exd4, use the c-file and the long diagonal, and pressure b2 with ...Qb6.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Tricks and traps"]
[Type "ideas"]
[FEN "r4rk1/pp1b1ppp/2nqp3/2Pp4/8/2PBP3/PP1N1PPP/R2QK2R b KQ - 0 12"]

{[%cal Rd3h7,Rd1h5,Rd2e4,Gd6c7] [%csl Rh7,Rc5] Eight patterns to know. The first three are White's traps in the main line with ...Nc6 (1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 Nf6 3.e3 c5 4.c3 Nc6 5.Nd2 e6 6.Ngf3 Bd6 7.Bg3 O-O 8.Bd3); our ...Qc7 and ...Nbd7 setup avoids them, but you can reach them by another move order.

Don't take on c5 with the queen. This position comes from 8...Qe7 9.Ne5 Nd7 10.Nxd7 Bxd7 11.Bxd6 Qxd6 12.dxc5. 12...Qxc5? loses to 13.Bxh7+! Kxh7 14.Qh5+ Kg8 15.Ne4, which hits the queen and threatens Ng5 and mate (Stockfish +3.4, winning for White). Play 12...Qc7 (Stockfish +0.5, slightly better for White).

Keep h4 guarded. After 8...b6 9.e4 dxe4 10.Nxe4 Nxe4 11.Bxe4 Bb7 12.dxc5 Bxc5 13.Qa4, the queen on d8 guards h4. 13...Qc8?? lets it go: 14.Bxh7+! Kxh7 15.Qh4+ Kg8 16.Ng5 (Stockfish +6.7, winning for White). Play 13...Rc8 14.Rd1 Qe7! (Stockfish +0.8, slightly better for White).

Watch the g3 bishop's diagonal. Instead of 8.Bd3, 8.Bb5 Ne7 9.Bd3 b6 10.e4 dxe4 11.Nxe4 Nxe4 12.Bxe4, the natural 12...Rb8? puts the rook on the bishop's diagonal. After 13.dxc5 your d6 bishop is attacked three times and can't take on c5 without dropping the rook (Stockfish +1.4, clearly better for White). 12...Nd5 holds (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).

Punish 8.dxe5?? in the King's Indian setup. After 2...g6 3.e3 Bg7 4.Nf3 O-O 5.Bd3 d6 6.c3 Nc6 7.Nbd2 e5, the natural 8.dxe5?? dxe5 wins a piece: the pawn hits the bishop on f4, and your queen hits the bishop on d3 down the open d-file (Stockfish −6.4, winning for Black). The King's Indian course has this as a puzzle.

Take on b2 only when it is safe. With your pawn on d5 and no knight on f6, ...Qxb2 against Nc3 is a blunder: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 c5 3.e3 Qb6 4.Nc3 Qxb2?? 5.Nxd5 (Stockfish +7.0, winning for White). With the knight on f6 it is playable but risky: 1.d4 Nf6 2.Bf4 c5 3.e3 Qb6 4.Nc3 Qxb2 5.Nb5 Nd5! is the only move (Stockfish +0.6, slightly better for White).

...Bf5 against Qc2 depends on the move order. With White's knight on f3 (1.d4 d5 2.Nf3 Nf6 3.Bf4 c5 4.e3 Nc6 5.c3 Qb6 6.Qb3 c4 7.Qc2), 7...Bf5! is good: 8.Qxf5?? Qxb2 wins the rook on a1, so White retreats with 8.Qc1 (Stockfish −0.3, slightly better for Black). With the knight on d2 instead, 7...Bf5?? 8.Qxf5 loses a piece.

The ...e4 fork. With White's bishop on d3 and knight on f3, a pawn on e4 hits both. In the main line, 9...e5 threatens it at once, so a quiet move such as 10.Bh4?? loses a piece to 10...e4! (Stockfish −6.0, winning for Black). In the King's Indian setup, after 8.Bg5 h6 9.Bh4 Qe7, the f6 knight is pinned, so ...e4 doesn't work yet. But after the natural 10.O-O?, 10...g5! breaks the pin, and 11.Bg3 e4 12.Nxg5 exd3 wins a piece (Stockfish −4.8, winning for Black).

A surprise weapon: 2...h5!?. Against 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4, it sets a trap for the automatic 3.e3??: 3...e5! hits the bishop, and ...g5 and ...h4 trap it (Stockfish −1.6, clearly better for Black). But after 3.Nf3 or 3.c4 the move is just weakening (Stockfish +0.7, slightly better for White), so keep it for blitz.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Move orders and the Jobava London"]
[Type "ideas"]

{White can reach the London by several move orders. Your setup stays the same; only the timing changes.

2.Nf3 first (1.d4 d5 2.Nf3 Nf6 3.Bf4). Play 3...e6 and 4...Bd6, as in this course. You also have a sharper option here: with White's knight already on f3, ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...Qb6 gain time, because after Qb3 c4 Qc2 your ...Bf5 comes with tempo instead of losing a piece (see Tricks and traps).

1...Nf6 players: 2.Nf3 g6 3.Bf4 Bg7 4.e3 O-O reaches the same King's Indian London. If White plays c4 at any point, you are back in the King's Indian course.

3.c4, a Queen's Gambit with the bishop on f4: play ...e6 and ...c5, or follow the Queen's Gambit course.

The Jobava London: the knight goes to c3 instead of d2, usually with Bf4 on move 2 or 3. White threatens Nb5 and Nxc7+, so watch c7. Step through the moves.}
1. d4 d5 2. Bf4 Nf6 3. Nc3 {[%cal Rc3b5,Rb5c7] This is the Rapport-Jobava System, usually called the Jobava London, after Baadur Jobava and Richárd Rapport, who made it popular. White wants Nb5 and Nxc7+, or e3, Qf3 and a quick attack.} e6 {Opens the f8 bishop's diagonal so ...Bb4 is possible.}
4. Nb5 {[%cal Rb5c7] Threatens 5.Nxc7+.} Na6 {[%cal Ga6c7] Guards c7. The knight returns to the game later via c7 or b8, and White's knight has nothing more to do on b5. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Main line: 5.Bg3"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Pawn Game: Accelerated London System"]
[ECO "D00"]

{Against 1...d5 2.Bf4, use the Queen's Gambit Declined pieces: ...Nf6, ...e6 and ...Bd6. White's main answer keeps the bishop with 5.Bg3. Complete the setup with ...c5, ...Qc7 and ...Nbd7, so that queen, bishop and knight all watch e5, then break with ...e5 once White has castled. White's other fifth moves are in the next chapter, and the ninth-move alternatives are under Advanced variations.}
1. d4 d5 2. Bf4 {This is the London System.}
(2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Bf4 {The London by another move order.} e6 4. e3 Bd6 {The same position as after 4...Bd6 in the main line. With White's knight already on f3 you also have a sharper plan with ...c5 and ...Qb6: see the trap chapter on ...Bf5 against Qc2. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.})
2... Nf6 {Develop first. The c-pawn can wait a move.}
3. e3 {3.Nf3 e6 4.e3 Bd6 comes to the same position. Against 3.Nc3, see the chapter Rapport-Jobava: 3.Nc3.} e6 {[%csl Yc8] The Queen's Gambit Declined setup. The c8 bishop stays home for now and guards b7, so White's Qb3 ideas don't work.}
4. Nf3 {4.Nd2 and 4.c3 usually come to the same positions after ...Bd6, ...O-O and ...c5. 4.Bd3 Bd6 5.Nf3 comes to the 5.Bd3 line in the next chapter. Against 4.c4, see Advanced variations.} Bd6 {[%cal Gd6f4] Offers to trade White's best minor piece.}
5. Bg3 {Keeps the bishop. Against 5.Ne5, castle, then play ...c5 and ...Qc7 to challenge the knight (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} O-O
6. Nbd2 {6.Bd3 and 6.c3 come to the same positions after ...c5.} c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4 from the side.}
7. c3 Qc7 {[%cal Gc7e5] The queen covers e5. If White takes on d6, the queen recaptures.}
8. Bd3 Nbd7 {[%cal Gd7e5] The knight covers e5 a third time. On d7 it is safe from Bb5, which could pin or trade a knight on c6.}
9. O-O {Against 9.e4, 9.Qe2, 9.Bxd6 and 9.c4, see Advanced variations.} e5 {[%cal Ge5e4] The break. It threatens ...e4, forking the knight on f3 and the bishop on d3, so White has to react at once.}
10. dxe5 {A quiet move such as 10.Bh4?? loses a piece to 10...e4!: see the trap chapter on 10.Bh4?? and ...e4. Against 10.e4, take with 10...exd4 11.cxd4 Bxg3 12.hxg3 dxe4 (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} Nxe5
11. Nxe5 {11.Bxe5 Bxe5 12.Nxe5 Qxe5 comes to the same position.} Bxe5 12. Bxe5 Qxe5 {[%cal Gc8g4,Gf8e8] The London bishop is gone, and your pieces are free. Next ...Bg4 or ...Bd7, and the rooks to the center. You may end up with an isolated pawn on d5, but with active pieces that is no problem. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "Other fifth moves: 5.Bxd6 and 5.Bd3"]
[Diagram "5.Bxd6"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Pawn Game: Accelerated London System"]
[ECO "D00"]

{White's other two main fifth moves. 5.Bxd6 trades the bishops at once: take back with the queen, castle, and play ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...e5. 5.Bd3 lets you take on f4, and White's pawns are doubled. Castle, then play ...b6, ...c5, ...a5 and ...Ba6 to trade off White's best attacking piece, the bishop on d3.}
1. d4 d5 2. Bf4 Nf6 3. e3 e6 4. Nf3 Bd6
5. Bxd6 {Trades the bishops at once.}
(5. Bd3 Bxf4 {Take it: White's pawns get doubled.} 6. exf4 {[%csl Yf2,Yf4] White's pawns are doubled, but the one on f4 controls e5, so a white knight can settle there.} O-O 7. O-O b6 {[%cal Gc8a6] Prepares ...Ba6, to trade the d3 bishop.} 8. Qe2 c5 9. c3 a5 {Prepares ...Ba6. Played at once, 9...Ba6?? loses a piece to 10.Bxa6 Nxa6 11.Qxa6. With the pawn on a5, the rook on a8 guards a6.} 10. Ne5 Ba6 11. Bxa6 Nxa6 {The light-squared bishops are gone, and the knight comes back into play via c7. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.})
5... Qxd6 {[%cal Gd6e5] The queen recaptures and already covers e5.}
6. Nbd2 {Against 6.c4, play 6...c5: after 7.cxd5 Nxd5 the game is open and level (Stockfish +0.2, equal).} O-O 7. Bd3 c5 8. c3 Nc6 {[%cal Gc6e5] Supports ...e5.}
9. dxc5 Qxc5 10. O-O e5 {[%cal Ge6e5] The break, and the center is yours.} 11. e4 Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Pins the knight that guards d4 and e5.} 12. h3 Bh5 {Keeps the pin. Black's pieces are active, and the queen on c5 eyes f2. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Rapport-Jobava: 3.Nc3"]
[Diagram "3.Nc3"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{The Jobava London: White puts the knight on c3 instead of d2. It blocks the c-pawn, but it threatens Nb5 and Nxc7+. Keep the ...e6 setup. Against e3, pin the knight with ...Bb4; against Nb5, guard c7 with ...Na6.}
1. d4 d5 2. Bf4 Nf6 3. Nc3 {This is the Rapport-Jobava System.} e6 {Opens the f8 bishop's diagonal, so ...Bb4 is possible.}
4. e3 {Against 4.Nf3, 4...c5 5.e3 a6 keeps the knight off b5 (Stockfish 0.0, equal).}
(4. Nb5 {[%cal Rb5c7] Threatens Nxc7+.} Na6 {[%cal Ga6c7] Guards c7.} 5. e3 Be7 6. Nf3 O-O 7. Bd3 c6 {Chases the knight from b5.} 8. Nc3 c5 {Now ...c5 hits the center, and the knight on a6 can come back via c7. Stockfish: −0.2, equal.})
4... Bb4 {[%cal Gb4c3] Pins the knight, so Nb5 is off for now.}
5. Nf3 O-O 6. Bd3 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits the center.} 7. dxc5 Nbd7 {Win the pawn back with the knight.}
8. O-O Bxc3 9. bxc3 Nxc5 {You have won the pawn back, and White's c-pawns are doubled. Stockfish: −0.3, slightly better for Black.} *

[Chapter "King's Indian setup: 5.h3 and 5.Be2"]
[Diagram "5.h3"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "London System"]
[ECO "A48"]

{If you play 1...Nf6 and the King's Indian, keep that setup: ...g6, ...Bg7, castle and ...d6. When White's bishop goes to e2, often with h3, ...e5 is too early, because dxe5 wins a pawn. Hit d4 with ...c5 instead, and answer c3 with ...Qb6. After Qb3, ...Be6 hits the queen, so White has to trade or retreat. After the trade, your rook gets the half-open a-file.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. Bf4 {This is the London System.}
(2. Nf3 g6 3. Bf4 Bg7 4. e3 O-O {The same position as after 4...O-O in the main line. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.})
2... g6 3. e3 {3.Nf3 Bg7 4.e3 O-O comes to the same position. Against 3.Nc3, see the chapter King's Indian setup: 3.Nc3.} Bg7 4. Nf3 O-O
5. h3 {[%csl Yh2] Gives the bishop a retreat on h2, so ...Nh5 can't trade it off, and stops ...Bg4. Against 5.c3, play 5...d6 and the same ideas.}
(5. Be2 d6 6. h3 c5 {The same position as after 6...c5 in the main line. Against 6.O-O, see Advanced variations. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.})
5... d6 6. Be2 {Against 6.Bd3, play 6...c5 or ...Nc6 and ...e5, as in the next chapter.} c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4.}
7. c3 {White's rule: c3 against ...c5.} Qb6 {[%cal Gb6b2] Hits b2.}
8. Qb3 Be6 {[%cal Ge6b3] Hits the queen, so White has to trade or retreat.}
9. Qxb6 axb6 {[%cal Ga8a2] The doubled b-pawns don't matter much here: the rook on a8 now hits a2.} 10. a3 Na6 {Heads for c7.}
11. Nbd2 cxd4 12. cxd4 Rfc8 {[%cal Gc8c1] Takes the open c-file.} 13. O-O Nd5 {[%cal Gd5f4] Hits the bishop. Your rooks have the half-open a-file and the open c-file. Stockfish: −0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "King's Indian setup: 5.Bd3"]
[Diagram "5.Bd3"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "London System"]
[ECO "A48"]

{When White's bishop goes to d3, ...Nc6 and ...e5 works well. Once the knight is on d2, it blocks the queen's protection of d3, so if the pawns are traded on e5, the d-file opens against the bishop. White has to keep the tension with Bg5, and even then you are at least equal. The same position comes up in the King's Indian course's page on the London.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. Bf4 g6 3. e3 Bg7 4. Nf3 O-O 5. Bd3 d6
6. c3 {Against 6.O-O, ...c5 is better than ...Nc6: with the knight still on b1, White's queen protects d3, and 6...Nc6 7.c3 e5 8.dxe5 dxe5 9.Nxe5 is good for White (Stockfish +0.6, slightly better for White). 6.h3 c5 is also fine.} Nc6
7. Nbd2 {[%csl Rd2,Rd3] Now the knight cuts the queen off from d3.} e5 {[%cal Ge5d4,Ge5f4] Now 8.dxe5?? loses a piece, and 8.d5?? loses too: see the trap chapter on 8.dxe5??.}
8. Bg5 {Keeps the tension and pins the knight.} h6 9. Bh4 Qe7 {Adds a guard to e5 and prepares ...g5, which breaks the pin. Now the natural 10.O-O? runs into 10...g5! and ...e4: see the trap chapter.}
10. Bc2 {Takes the bishop off d3, so ...e4 no longer forks anything.} exd4 {[%cal Ge7e3] Opens the e-file.} 11. Nxd4 Nxd4 12. cxd4 Re8 {[%cal Ge8e3] The rook and queen press on e3. White's king is still in the center. Stockfish: −0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "King's Indian setup: 3.Nc3"]
[Diagram "3.Nc3"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{3.Nc3 prepares e4, which would give White a big center. Stop it with 3...d5, and you have a Grünfeld setup: ...g6, ...Bg7 and ...d5. Castle, then hit the center with ...c5. Against Nb5, guard c7 with ...Na6 (see the trap chapter on Nb5 and Nxc7+). Against h4, answer ...h5 at once.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. Bf4 g6 3. Nc3 {This is the Rapport-Jobava System.} d5 {Stops e4. After 3...Bg7 4.e4, White gets a big center (Stockfish +0.6).}
4. e3 {Against 4.Nb5, play 4...Na6: see the trap chapter. 4.Nf3 Bg7 5.e3 O-O comes to the main line.} Bg7
5. Nf3 {Against 5.Nb5, play 5...Na6: see the trap chapter.}
(5. h4 {[%cal Rh4h5] White wants h5 to open the h-file.} h5 {[%csl Yh5] Stops h5 for good.} 6. Nf3 O-O 7. Be2 c5 8. dxc5 Qa5 9. O-O Qxc5 {You have won the pawn back, and White's pawn on h4 is just a weakness. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.})
5... O-O 6. Be2 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits the center.} 7. dxc5 Qa5 {[%cal Ga5c5] The queen will win the pawn back.}
8. Nd2 {Heads for b3 to hit the queen.} Qxc5 9. Nb3 Qb6 10. O-O Nc6 {You have won the pawn back and developed. Next ...Rd8 and ...e5 or ...Bf5. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "Main line: White's ninth moves"]
[Diagram "9.e4"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Pawn Game: Accelerated London System"]
[ECO "D00"]

{Four other ninth moves after 8...Nbd7. Against the break 9.e4, trade on d4 and e4: White is left with an isolated pawn on d4. Against 9.Bxd6 and 9.Qe2, play ...e5 at once. Against 9.c4, trade on d4 and g3, then take on c4: again White gets an isolated d-pawn.}
1. d4 d5 2. Bf4 Nf6 3. e3 e6 4. Nf3 Bd6 5. Bg3 O-O 6. Nbd2 c5 7. c3 Qc7 8. Bd3 Nbd7
9. e4 {[%csl Re4] White breaks first.}
(9. Bxd6 Qxd6 10. O-O e5 {The break comes at once.} 11. Nxe5 Nxe5 12. dxe5 Qxe5 13. Re1 Bg4 {[%cal Gg4d1] Develops with a threat to the queen. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.})
(9. Qe2 e5 10. dxe5 Nxe5 11. Bxe5 Bxe5 12. Nxe5 Qxe5 13. O-O Re8 {[%cal Ge8e3] The queens are still on, and your pieces are free. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.})
(9. c4 cxd4 10. exd4 Bxg3 11. hxg3 dxc4 {After 12.Nxc4, White's d-pawn is isolated. Blockade it with a knight on d5. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.})
9... cxd4 {Opens the c-file before you take on e4.} 10. cxd4 dxe4 11. Bxe4 {11.Nxe4 Nxe4 12.Bxe4 Nf6 is even better for you (Stockfish −0.7, slightly better for Black).} Nxe4
12. Nxe4 Bb4+ {[%cal Gb4e1] Check, so White's king has to deal with it before castling.} 13. Nfd2 Qb6 {[%cal Gb6d4] The isolated pawn on d4 is under fire. Stockfish: −0.4, slightly better for Black.} *

[Chapter "Queen's Gambit move orders: 3.c4 and 4.c4"]
[Diagram "4.c4"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Pawn Game: Accelerated London System"]
[ECO "D00"]

{When White plays c4, the game becomes a Queen's Gambit with White's bishop on f4. Hit the center with ...c5, as against the Harrwitz Attack in the Queen's Gambit course. If White takes on c5, take back with the bishop.}
1. d4 d5 2. Bf4 Nf6 3. e3
(3. c4 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4 at once.} 4. dxc5 e6 5. Nf3 Bxc5 6. e3 O-O {You have won the pawn back and developed quickly. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.})
3... e6 4. c4 {This is a Queen's Gambit now.} c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] The same idea. 4...Bb4+ is also good (Stockfish −0.1, equal).}
5. Nc3 cxd4 6. exd4 Bb4 {[%cal Gb4c3] Pins the knight and puts pressure on d4.} 7. Nf3 O-O {Castle next to the pin. White will have an isolated pawn after cxd5. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "King's Indian setup: White's sixth moves"]
[Diagram "6.O-O"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "London System, with Be2"]
[ECO "A48"]

{When White castles early instead of playing h3, the bishop on f4 has no retreat on h2. Hit it with ...Nh5. Against 5.Bd3 and 6.O-O, play ...c5 rather than ...Nc6 and ...e5, because White's queen still guards d3.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. Bf4 g6 3. e3 Bg7 4. Nf3 O-O 5. Be2
(5. Bd3 d6 6. O-O c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4.} 7. c3 Nc6 8. Bg3 Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Pins the knight.} 9. h3 Bxf3 {You give up the bishop, but White's d4 pawn loses a defender. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.})
5... d6 6. O-O Nh5 {[%cal Gh5f4] Hits the bishop.} 7. Bg5 h6 8. Bh4 g5 {[%cal Gg5h4] Chases the bishop again.}
9. Nfd2 {Opens the bishop's diagonal from e2 to h5: if 9...gxh4, 10.Bxh5 wins the knight back. 9.Bg3 Nxg3 gives you the bishop pair.} Nf4! {[%cal Gf4e2] Gets the knight out of the bishop's way and hits the bishop on e2.}
10. exf4 gxh4 {Take back at once: other moves let the bishop escape, and White stays a piece up. Now you have the bishop pair. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Trap: 10.Bh4?? and ...e4"]
[Diagram "10.Bh4"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Queen's Pawn Game: Accelerated London System"]
[ECO "D00"]

{In the main line, 9...e5 threatens ...e4, which forks the knight on f3 and the bishop on d3. White has to react at once, with 10.dxe5, 10.Nxe5 or 10.e4. 10.Bh4 looks active, since it hits the knight on f6, but it ignores the threat. Quiet moves such as 10.Re1 or 10.Qe2 lose a piece the same way.}
1. d4 d5 2. Bf4 Nf6 3. e3 e6 4. Nf3 Bd6 5. Bg3 O-O 6. Nbd2 c5 7. c3 Qc7 8. Bd3 Nbd7 9. O-O e5
10. Bh4?? {Hits the knight on f6, but ignores the threat.} e4! {[%cal Ge4f3,Ge4d3] Forks the knight and the bishop.}
11. dxc5 {White's best try: it grabs a pawn.} Bxc5 12. Bc2 exf3 {You have won the knight. After 13.Nxf3 you are a piece for a pawn up. Stockfish: −5.9, winning for Black.} *

[Chapter "Trap: ...Bf5 against Qc2"]
[Diagram "7...Bf5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Queen's Pawn Game: London System"]
[ECO "D02"]

{A sharp option against the 2.Nf3 move order. With White's knight on f3 instead of d2, ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...Qb6 gain time on b2. After Qb3 c4 Qc2, ...Bf5 hits the queen, and taking the bishop loses the rook on a1. With the knight on d2, the same ...Bf5 would lose a piece, so check where White's knight is.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Bf4 c5 4. e3 Nc6
5. c3 {Against 5.Nbd2, play 5...e6 and ...Bd6 (Stockfish +0.2, equal), and watch the Greek gift traps on the Tricks and traps page.} Qb6 {[%cal Gb6b2] Hits b2.}
6. Qb3 c4 {[%cal Gc4b3] Hits the queen.} 7. Qc2 Bf5! {[%cal Gf5c2] Hits the queen again.}
8. Qxf5?? {Takes the bishop, and loses the rook.}
(8. Qc1 {The right retreat: the queen guards b2.} e6 {Develop and castle. White's queen on c1 is passive. Stockfish: −0.4, slightly better for Black.})
8... Qxb2 {[%cal Gb2a1,Gb2b1] Hits the rook on a1 and the knight on b1.} 9. Be2 e6 10. Qh3 Qxa1 {You have won a rook and a pawn for the bishop, and the knight on b1 is still stuck. Stockfish: −6.3, winning for Black.} *

[Chapter "Trap: 2...h5!? and 3.e3??"]
[Diagram "2...h5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Queen's Pawn Game: Accelerated London System"]
[ECO "D00"]

{A surprise weapon for blitz. 2...h5 sets a trap for the automatic 3.e3??: after 3...e5!, the pawns on g5 and h4 trap the f4 bishop. Against the best replies, 3.Nf3 or 3.c4, the move only weakens your kingside (Stockfish +0.7, slightly better for White).}
1. d4 d5 2. Bf4 h5!? {[%cal Gh5h4] [%engine ok] Prepares ...e5, ...g5 and ...h4 against the bishop.}
3. e3?? {The automatic London move, and it walks into the trap.}
(3. Nf3 {Guards e5, so the trap is off.} Nf6 4. e3 Bf5 5. c4 e6 {You have a normal position, but the pawn on h5 is a weakness. Stockfish: +0.6, slightly better for White.})
3... e5! {[%cal Ge5f4] Hits the bishop. 4.Bxe5 f6 limits the damage (Stockfish −1.6, clearly better for Black).}
4. dxe5? g5! {[%cal Gg5f4] Hits the bishop again.} 5. Bg3? {The bishop is lost anyway. Giving it up at once with 5.Bxg5 Qxg5 or 5.Nf3 gxf4 limits the damage (Stockfish −3.0, winning for Black).} h4 {[%cal Gh4g3] The bishop is trapped (Stockfish −4.4, winning for Black).}
6. Bxh4 gxh4 {You have won the bishop for two pawns. Stockfish: −4.5, winning for Black.} *

[Chapter "Trap: Nb5 and Nxc7+"]
[Diagram "4.Nb5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{In the Jobava London, White's knight on c3 and bishop on f4 both aim at c7. When the knight jumps to b5, guard c7 at once with ...Na6. The natural ...Bg7 or ...O-O loses a rook, or at least the exchange, to Nxc7.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. Bf4 g6 3. Nc3 d5
4. Nb5 {[%cal Rb5c7,Rf4c7] Two pieces hit c7.}
(4. e3 Bg7 5. Nb5 {The same idea a move later.} Na6! {[%cal Ga6c7] Guards c7.} (5... O-O?? 6. Nxc7 {The knight takes the pawn and hits the rook on a8. White wins the exchange. Stockfish: +5.5, winning for White.}) 6. Nc3 Nb8 {The knights go back, and the game is level. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.})
4... Na6! {[%cal Ga6c7] Guards c7.}
(4... Bg7?? 5. Nxc7+ Kf8 6. Nxa8 {White has won the rook. Stockfish: +6.8, winning for White.})
5. e3 Bg7 6. Nc3 {The knight has nothing more to do on b5.} Nb8 {The same position as after 4.e3 Bg7 in the chapter King's Indian setup: 3.Nc3. The knight returns to b8 and can go to c6 or d7 later. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Trap: 8.dxe5?? in the King's Indian setup"]
[Diagram "8.dxe5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "London System"]
[ECO "A48"]

{Against 5.Bd3, ...Nc6 and ...e5 sets a trap. Once White's knight is on d2, it cuts the queen off from the bishop on d3. If White takes on e5, the d-file opens, and your pawn and queen hit both bishops at once. The King's Indian course shows the same trap on its London page.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. Bf4 g6 3. e3 Bg7 4. Nf3 O-O 5. Bd3 d6 6. c3 Nc6 7. Nbd2 e5
8. dxe5?? {The natural capture, and it loses a piece.}
(8. d5?? {Hits the knight, but} exf4! {[%cal Gf4e3] Take the bishop first.} 9. dxc6 fxe3 10. fxe3 Qe8 {[%cal Ge8e3,Ge8c6] The queen hits e3 and c6, and White's king is stuck in the center. Stockfish: −4.7, winning for Black.})
8... dxe5 {[%cal Ge5f4,Gd8d3] Both bishops are attacked: the pawn hits f4 and the queen hits d3 (Stockfish −6.4, winning for Black).}
9. Nxe5 {Saves one bishop by removing the e5 pawn.} Nxe5 10. Bxe5 Qxd3 {You have won a piece for a pawn. Stockfish: −6.4, winning for Black.} *

[Chapter "Trap: 10.O-O? g5! and ...e4"]
[Diagram "10.O-O"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "London System"]
[ECO "A48"]

{In the 5.Bd3 line, after 8.Bg5 h6 9.Bh4 Qe7, the bishop on h4 pins the f6 knight to your queen, so ...e4 at once fails: 10.O-O e4? 11.Nxe4 (Stockfish +3.4, winning for White). But once White has castled, ...g5 breaks the pin. Then the knight guards e4 again, and ...e4 forks the knight on f3 and the bishop on d3.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. Bf4 g6 3. e3 Bg7 4. Nf3 O-O 5. Bd3 d6 6. c3 Nc6 7. Nbd2 e5 8. Bg5 h6 9. Bh4 Qe7
10. O-O? {Natural, but it allows the trick. 10.Bc2 is the right move.} g5! {[%cal Gg5h4] Hits the bishop and breaks the pin.}
11. Bg3? {The natural retreat, and it loses a piece.}
(11. Nxg5 {White's best: a knight for two pawns, to pin the f6 knight.} hxg5 12. Bxg5 d5! {Stockfish's choice: it takes e4 away from White. You have a knight for two pawns. Stockfish: −1.5, clearly better for Black.})
11... e4! {[%cal Ge4f3,Ge4d3] Forks the knight and the bishop.} 12. Nxg5 exd3 {You have won a piece for a pawn. Stockfish: −4.8, winning for Black.} *
